WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.780
Inventory and reporting are natural starting points for Windows administration automation because they produce visibility without immediately changing systems.

2
00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:22.520
A well-scoped inventory script might collect computer identity, operating system version, installed software, running services, key processes, local users and groups, disk space, network configuration, and recent event summaries.

3
00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:26.460
The exact data should match the operational question being answered.

4
00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:28.340
Structured output matters.

5
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:41.640
When scripts emit objects, administrators can filter by service status, sort by free disk space, export to CSV or JSON, compare snapshots over time, or feed results into another workflow.

6
00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:47.580
Unstructured text is sometimes fine for human reading, but it becomes brittle when other scripts depend on it.

7
00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:53.600
Production reporting should prefer predictable properties and clear names so the output can survive reuse.

8
00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.680
Inventory scripts also need data minimization.

9
00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:03.020
It is tempting to collect everything because it may be useful someday, but broad collection can create new sensitive datasets.

10
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:12.240
Reports may include usernames, hostnames, software inventories, network details, file paths, event records, and operational clues.

11
00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:21.380
Before exporting or retaining results, ask who needs the report, where it will be stored, how long it will exist, and whether sensitive fields are necessary.

12
00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:26.620
Good reporting scripts make collection failures visible without collapsing the entire run.

13
00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:34.640
One host may be offline, one registry location may be unavailable, or one query may require permissions the operator does not have.

14
00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:42.260
A useful script records which systems succeeded, which failed, why they failed, and whether the failure affects trust in the report.

15
00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.960
Reliable inventory is not only about gathering data.

16
00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:48.460
It is about making the limits of that data clear.
